{"type":"message","id":"44954507","parentId":"c73a05cc","timestamp":"2026-04-10T17:01:11.544Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_j3z6vRzL2PmDrmHX4qQNXN1a|fc_018f1b0fb3360e1d0169d92cd71b6c81919079c51368569ee9","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: llm-distillator\ndescription: Distill health-related conversation context plus diagnostic tool output into a clear, comprehensive patient-facing answer using a Hugging Face-hosted Llama 70B model. Use when the agent needs to (1) extract clinically relevant context from the current conversation, including symptoms, history, red flags, timelines, and other useful health details, (2) combine that context with the output of a diagnosis or screening tool, and (3) produce a concise but complete explanation that is easy for a user to understand.\n---\n\n# Llm Distillator\n\n## Overview\n\nUse this skill to synthesize two inputs into one final explanation: the health-relevant conversation context and the output of a diagnostic tool. The bundled script calls a Hugging Face chat-completions endpoint with a Llama 70B instruction model and returns a clean, user-facing summary.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Extract only the health-relevant parts of the current conversation.\n2. Gather the diagnostic or screening tool output exactly as produced.\n3. Run `scripts/distill_health_response.py` with both inputs.\n4. Review the generated answer for safety, uncertainty, and emergency red flags before sending it.\n\n## Extract conversation context\n\nInclude only information that helps clinical interpretation or next-step guidance:\n\n- Current symptoms and severity\n- Onset, duration, and timeline\n- Relevant negatives and positives\n- Prior diagnoses, tests, medications, allergies, and risk factors if present\n- The user’s explicit concerns or goals\n- Contradictions between subjective report and tool output\n\nDo not pass unrelated small talk or irrelevant project chatter.\n\n## Prepare tool output\n\nPreserve the diagnostic result as faithfully as possible. Include:\n\n- The exact tool name if known\n- Raw output or structured result\n- Confidence, classification, or score if present\n- Any caveats or limitations already reported by the tool\n\n## Run the script\n\nBasic usage:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/distill_health_response.py \\\n  --context-file /tmp/health_context.txt \\\n  --diagnosis-file /tmp/diagnosis_output.txt\n```\n\nDirect text usage:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/distill_health_response.py \\\n  --context \"Patient reports right flank pain for 2 weeks, intermittent blood in urine, no fever.\" \\\n  --diagnosis \"Kidney scan classifier output: suspicious lesion detected, confidence 0.81.\"\n```\n\nOptional flags:\n\n- `--model`: override the default Hugging Face model\n- `--temperature`: default `0.2`\n- `--max-tokens`: default `700`\n- `--print-prompt`: print the assembled prompt instead of calling the API\n- `--output-file`: save the final distilled answer to a file\n\n## Output requirements\n\nThe desired output should:\n\n- Be clear and readable for a non-expert\n- Combine conversation context and tool output into one narrative\n- Distinguish findings from uncertainty\n- Avoid overstating certainty\n- Mention urgent escalation when the combined picture suggests red flags\n- End with practical next-step guidance when appropriate\n\n## Safety rules\n\nAlways review the generated output before using it.\n\n- Do not treat the LLM answer as an independent diagnosis.\n- Preserve uncertainty if the source tool was uncertain.\n- If emergency symptoms are present, prioritize urgent evaluation language.\n- If the conversation context conflicts with the diagnostic output, say so plainly.\n- Do not invent lab values, timelines, or findings that were not present in the inputs.\n\n## Bundled resources\n\n- `scripts/distill_health_response.py`: calls the Hugging Face chat-completions API with a Llama 70B model.\n- `references/prompt-contract.md`: defines the expected input and output contract for consistent distillation."}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":13,"aggregated":"---\nname: llm-distillator\ndescription: Distill health-related conversation context plus diagnostic tool output into a clear, comprehensive patient-facing answer using a Hugging Face-hosted Llama 70B model. Use when the agent needs to (1) extract clinically relevant context from the current conversation, including symptoms, history, red flags, timelines, and other useful health details, (2) combine that context with the output of a diagnosis or screening tool, and (3) produce a concise but complete explanation that is easy for a user to understand.\n---\n\n# Llm Distillator\n\n## Overview\n\nUse this skill to synthesize two inputs into one final explanation: the health-relevant conversation context and the output of a diagnostic tool. The bundled script calls a Hugging Face chat-completions endpoint with a Llama 70B instruction model and returns a clean, user-facing summary.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Extract only the health-relevant parts of the current conversation.\n2. Gather the diagnostic or screening tool output exactly as produced.\n3. Run `scripts/distill_health_response.py` with both inputs.\n4. Review the generated answer for safety, uncertainty, and emergency red flags before sending it.\n\n## Extract conversation context\n\nInclude only information that helps clinical interpretation or next-step guidance:\n\n- Current symptoms and severity\n- Onset, duration, and timeline\n- Relevant negatives and positives\n- Prior diagnoses, tests, medications, allergies, and risk factors if present\n- The user’s explicit concerns or goals\n- Contradictions between subjective report and tool output\n\nDo not pass unrelated small talk or irrelevant project chatter.\n\n## Prepare tool output\n\nPreserve the diagnostic result as faithfully as possible. Include:\n\n- The exact tool name if known\n- Raw output or structured result\n- Confidence, classification, or score if present\n- Any caveats or limitations already reported by the tool\n\n## Run the script\n\nBasic usage:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/distill_health_response.py \\\n  --context-file /tmp/health_context.txt \\\n  --diagnosis-file /tmp/diagnosis_output.txt\n```\n\nDirect text usage:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/distill_health_response.py \\\n  --context \"Patient reports right flank pain for 2 weeks, intermittent blood in urine, no fever.\" \\\n  --diagnosis \"Kidney scan classifier output: suspicious lesion detected, confidence 0.81.\"\n```\n\nOptional flags:\n\n- `--model`: override the default Hugging Face model\n- `--temperature`: default `0.2`\n- `--max-tokens`: default `700`\n- `--print-prompt`: print the assembled prompt instead of calling the API\n- `--output-file`: save the final distilled answer to a file\n\n## Output requirements\n\nThe desired output should:\n\n- Be clear and readable for a non-expert\n- Combine conversation context and tool output into one narrative\n- Distinguish findings from uncertainty\n- Avoid overstating certainty\n- Mention urgent escalation when the combined picture suggests red flags\n- End with practical next-step guidance when appropriate\n\n## Safety rules\n\nAlways review the generated output before using it.\n\n- Do not treat the LLM answer as an independent diagnosis.\n- Preserve uncertainty if the source tool was uncertain.\n- If emergency symptoms are present, prioritize urgent evaluation language.\n- If the conversation context conflicts with the diagnostic output, say so plainly.\n- Do not invent lab values, timelines, or findings that were not present in the inputs.\n\n## Bundled resources\n\n- `scripts/distill_health_response.py`: calls the Hugging Face chat-completions API with a Llama 70B model.\n- `references/prompt-contract.md`: defines the expected input and output contract for consistent distillation.","cwd":"/home/node/.openclaw/workspace-demo-agent"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1775840471538}}
{"type":"message","id":"6158e8d0","parentId":"44954507","timestamp":"2026-04-10T17:01:34.104Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_018f1b0fb3360e1d0169d92ce0392c8191a724497226303c57\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"encrypted_content\":\"gAAAAABp2Szm6jqvX2CgXbsF5tZ28QxsuJ3bY-LB6CHiidg9FyGgY1l3vnAQddhJOoFqO-sFIh-WHOM8dNjjGAIK_f0SXD-hpNcsKiidtf7FDCSS56X58oRIxqe6B9sZrICivd4ZyLcDmYs-uxewoNyzHDqwHuGDYASxDFRLTSYGyZhAM0wfvmEkq_mUV--2KLlen6AXoubg3YVy9vDY39ssus3965phFtVSjLzWaz2DxALV0PUNlDPCAcLRfyljHLNICt4ikRSkwOcWPVBbhm5famSjLQsNScyhZq8u387-3YHiJ175sQCqJpIjg8vV4_Lx21op1UrjWh_s0v7NyLYatRlWv9h8Oq2mAGXz-LAu2gSkvglWiF3edn673zh0ovw4_AbX_C-Il7Hr4NByVyD9UCKMCaB_Yu7u2xMBkIs87DD19ojNPbQ6zMBC0RcxyuIy-fd6uD7fwRo2Z6ko2YZBf_2VcRQS12FDBVMB_9FbLx4d_ze8pBYHEKR8tPHY3ERJojN67iiS9ycVAyWP2_uR5VZHyRNvQpPXLAB1_t1dwUIPt7BWIhrMkG1qOuvze8m6bm1QVuTCzIp_nR6Klm42wR4ZInQPx-WF793YvUj37IDRIPtfBVVObuo13qAsjRLg8RP61ktTqdU86OXGPdTf0sJSBC7RAyCboh5capV_-U-xNzSnhPRx-jvnkK5csihXBr6y9y_mraVMxa_u2OYif9Vws4KYIehy1YMtUNYG6Z8S4UQjbyHrc_2GwS5mGgdUn3H8bFvDTNphbO4TeDNpFXOvQm3XSK3hcPl-QJxYZ0_QqVlFaSnEyUUjq3md8Z6WcDoo9m2dm2wv32o_DcCp97XZaWO97uqYo6Ksvlp7LO8hJAb9p_s=\",\"summary\":[]}"},{"type":"text","text":"I’ll combine the symptom history and the kidney pipeline result into one clear medical summary, then I’ll review it before sending.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_018f1b0fb3360e1d0169d92ce6519481918bde31f9c002b6ae\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_qI2HKlbBThYrAfVN8shHcO72|fc_018f1b0fb3360e1d0169d92ce66544819194c2e76cb87e5ffb","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cat > /tmp/health_context.txt <<'EOF'\nPatient reports feeling quite exhausted lately.\nPersistent dull ache in the lower back on the right side, worsening over the last few months.\nUser says urine is at minimum pinkish and sometimes red (visible hematuria).\nUser reports burning when urinating and compelling urgency/effort to urinate.\nPain is somehow constant and increases during evenings and nights.\nUser reports unintentional weight loss of 23 kg in the last 3 months.\nRelevant structured patient context from patient KLM (P-001): recent fatigue, mild hematuria, creatinine 1.4 mg/dL, eGFR 61 mL/min/1.73m², prior renal cell carcinoma follow-up, post-nephrectomy status, ongoing sunitinib 50 mg daily, monitoring eGFR decline, family history of renal cell carcinoma in maternal uncle.\nClinical concern already identified: visible blood in urine, persistent right-sided flank/back pain, fatigue, dysuria/urgency, major weight loss, prior RCC history.\nGoal: patient-facing summary of what this combination may mean and what next steps are needed.\nEOF\n\ncat > /tmp/diagnosis_output.txt <<'EOF'\nTool: kidney-pipeline-execution\nKidney Cancer Detector:\n- class: kidney_tumor\n- confidence: 0.9999004602432251\n- inference time: 8.863 s\n\nImage Analyzer:\n- prediction: kidney_tumor\n- cam_metrics.coverage: 0.03220663265306122\n- cam_metrics.center_ratio: 0.049504950490148025\n- cam_metrics.lr_asym: 0.5445544553916283\n- cam_metrics.tb_asym: 0.9999999999009902\n- cam_explanation: The model focused on a localized region of the image, located toward the peripheral renal regions, with asymmetric spatial distribution. In the context of a tumor prediction, this pattern suggests visually distinct renal regions that may correspond to localized structural alteration.\n\nX-AI:\n- confidence_interpretation: The model's confidence level of 99.99% suggests that the prediction is highly reliable, but it is essential to consider other diagnostic information and medical literature for a comprehensive understanding.\n- summary: The provided medical image is likely to be related to kidney cancer based on the model's prediction and confidence level.\n- visual_evidence: The image appears to show a kidney tumor, which is consistent with the model's prediction of kidney cancer.\n- recommended_next_steps:\n  1. Consult medical literature on kidney cancer, including its symptoms, diagnosis, and treatment options.\n  2. Review the patient's medical history and perform additional diagnostic tests to confirm the diagnosis.\n  3. Consider imaging studies, such as CT or MRI scans, to further evaluate the tumor's size and location.\n\nImportant caveat: This pipeline output is a preliminary signal only and not a confirmed diagnosis.\nEOF\n\npython3 ~/.openclaw/skills/llm-distillator/scripts/distill_health_response.py --context-file /tmp/health_context.txt --diagnosis-file /tmp/diagnosis_output.txt","workdir":"/home/node/.openclaw/workspace-demo-agent","yieldMs":1000,"timeout":120},"partialJson":"{\"command\":\"cat > /tmp/health_context.txt <<'EOF'\\nPatient reports feeling quite exhausted lately.\\nPersistent dull ache in the lower back on the right side, worsening over the last few months.\\nUser says urine is at minimum pinkish and sometimes red (visible hematuria).\\nUser reports burning when urinating and compelling urgency/effort to urinate.\\nPain is somehow constant and increases during evenings and nights.\\nUser reports unintentional weight loss of 23 kg in the last 3 months.\\nRelevant structured patient context from patient KLM (P-001): recent fatigue, mild hematuria, creatinine 1.4 mg/dL, eGFR 61 mL/min/1.73m², prior renal cell carcinoma follow-up, post-nephrectomy status, ongoing sunitinib 50 mg daily, monitoring eGFR decline, family history of renal cell carcinoma in maternal uncle.\\nClinical concern already identified: visible blood in urine, persistent right-sided flank/back pain, fatigue, dysuria/urgency, major weight loss, prior RCC history.\\nGoal: patient-facing summary of what this combination may mean and what next steps are needed.\\nEOF\\n\\ncat > /tmp/diagnosis_output.txt <<'EOF'\\nTool: kidney-pipeline-execution\\nKidney Cancer Detector:\\n- class: kidney_tumor\\n- confidence: 0.9999004602432251\\n- inference time: 8.863 s\\n\\nImage Analyzer:\\n- prediction: kidney_tumor\\n- cam_metrics.coverage: 0.03220663265306122\\n- cam_metrics.center_ratio: 0.049504950490148025\\n- cam_metrics.lr_asym: 0.5445544553916283\\n- cam_metrics.tb_asym: 0.9999999999009902\\n- cam_explanation: The model focused on a localized region of the image, located toward the peripheral renal regions, with asymmetric spatial distribution. In the context of a tumor prediction, this pattern suggests visually distinct renal regions that may correspond to localized structural alteration.\\n\\nX-AI:\\n- confidence_interpretation: The model's confidence level of 99.99% suggests that the prediction is highly reliable, but it is essential to consider other diagnostic information and medical literature for a comprehensive understanding.\\n- summary: The provided medical image is likely to be related to kidney cancer based on the model's prediction and confidence level.\\n- visual_evidence: The image appears to show a kidney tumor, which is consistent with the model's prediction of kidney cancer.\\n- recommended_next_steps:\\n  1. Consult medical literature on kidney cancer, including its symptoms, diagnosis, and treatment options.\\n  2. Review the patient's medical history and perform additional diagnostic tests to confirm the diagnosis.\\n  3. Consider imaging studies, such as CT or MRI scans, to further evaluate the tumor's size and location.\\n\\nImportant caveat: This pipeline output is a preliminary signal only and not a confirmed diagnosis.\\nEOF\\n\\npython3 ~/.openclaw/skills/llm-distillator/scripts/distill_health_response.py --context-file /tmp/health_context.txt --diagnosis-file /tmp/diagnosis_output.txt\",\"workdir\":\"/home/node/.openclaw/workspace-demo-agent\",\"yieldMs\":1000,\"timeout\":120}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":935,"output":764,"cacheRead":29824,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":31523,"cost":{"input":0.0023375,"output":0.01146,"cacheRead":0.007456,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0212535}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timest