LST5n3vvQpT55w=\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Addressing tool unavailability**\\n\\nI need to inform the user that the tool isn't available, so I should provide a manual, distilled answer instead. It seems there are access restrictions preventing me from running the skill. After I give the summary, I should check in with the user to see if they're satisfied or if they need anything else. Since I didn't actually run the tool, I can still offer a concise summary based on what I know.\"}]}"},{"type":"text","text":"[[reply_to_current]] **Distilled medical summary**\nYou have a combination of symptoms and findings that is **concerning for a significant kidney/urinary tract problem** that needs **urgent in-person evaluation**. The key pieces are: **persistent right-sided lower back/flank pain**, **visible blood in the urine**, **burning and urgency with urination**, **marked fatigue**, and **23 kg of unintentional weight loss over 3 months**. Your prior record also includes **renal cell carcinoma follow-up**, **post-nephrectomy status**, mildly reduced kidney function, and ongoing **sunitinib** treatment, which raises the importance of not dismissing this as routine back pain or a simple urinary irritation.\n\n**How the CT pipeline fits**\nThe kidney imaging pipeline flagged the scan as **kidney_tumor** with **very high confidence**, and the image analyzer also highlighted a **localized asymmetric renal area** that the tool considered suspicious. This supports concern, but it is still **only a preliminary tool signal**, not a confirmed diagnosis. It cannot by itself determine whether this is **tumor recurrence, another renal mass, obstruction, stone disease, infection, or another complication**. That distinction needs a clinician, proper imaging review, labs, and examination.\n\n**Most important next step**\nGiven the full picture, the safest course is **urgent medical assessment today or as soon as possible** through your **oncologist, urologist, or the ER** if rapid access is not available. The combination of **gross hematuria + weight loss + one-sided persistent pain + prior kidney cancer history** is enough to justify prompt escalation.\n\n**What doctors will likely want**\nThey will likely need:\n- **formal radiology review of the CT**\n- **urinalysis and urine culture**\n- **CBC**\n- **kidney function blood tests**\n- possible **repeat/targeted CT or MRI**\n- urgent **oncology/urology follow-up**\n\n**When to go to the ER immediately**\nGo immediately if you develop:\n- heavier bleeding or clots in urine\n- fever or chills\n- severe worsening pain\n- vomiting\n- inability to pass urine\n- dizziness, fainting, or major weakness\n\nI couldn’t run the dedicated LLM distillation tool directly because the external service was blocked, so I produced this distilled summary for you here instead.\n\nIf you want, I can now turn this into a **short doctor/ER handoff message** you can copy and send. Are you satisfied with this, or do you need something else?","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_018f1b0fb3360e1d0169d92cfc1634819186c752641882973d\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":1878,"output":589,"cacheRead":31360,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":33827,"cost":{"input":0.004695,"output":0.008835,"cacheRead":0.00784,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.02137}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1775840494369}}
